"[2511.13653] Weight-sparse transformers have interpretable circuits (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.13653)\nciteturn2141view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2138view0\",\"id\":5}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 17 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:Weight-sparse transformers have interpretable circuits\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Leo Gao , cite6†Achyuta Rajaram , cite7†Jacob Coxon , cite8†Soham V. Govande , cite9†Bowen Baker , cite10†Dan Mossing L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Weight-sparse transformers have interpretable circuits, by Leo Gao and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Finding human-understandable circuits in language models is a central goal of the field of mechanistic interpretability. We train models to have more understandable circuits by constraining most of their weights to be zeros, so that each neuron only has a few connections. To recover fine-grained circuits underlying each of several hand-crafted tasks, we prune the models to isolate the part responsible for the task.\nL17: These circuits often contain neurons and residual channels that correspond to natural concepts, with a small number of straightforwardly interpretable connections between them. We study how these models scale and find that making weights sparser trades off capability for interpretability, and scaling model size improves the capability-interpretability frontier. However, scaling sparse models beyond tens of millions of nonzero parameters while preserving interpretability remains a challenge.\nL18: In addition to training weight-sparse models de novo, we show preliminary results suggesting our method can also be adapted to explain existing dense models. Our work produces circuits that achieve an unprecedented level of human understandability and validates them with considerable rigor.\nL19: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2511.13653 [cs.LG]\nL21:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2511.13653v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL22:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.13653†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Leo Gao [cite16†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Mon, 17 Nov 2025 18:02:06 UTC (3,766 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Weight-sparse transformers have interpretable circuits, by Leo Gao and 5 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite11†View PDF L35:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite17†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite18†view license†creativecommons.org L39: \nL40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.LG\nL43: \nL44: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L45: \nL46: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2025-11 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite24†cs L51: cite25†cs.AI L52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL71: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL74: \nL75: Bibliographic Explorer (cite29†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL78: \nL79: Connected Papers (cite30†What is C